================================================================================
Markovian Coalescent Simulator (MaCS)
================================================================================

Files:      src/algorithm.cpp
            src/constants.h
            src/datastructures.cpp
            src/simulator.cpp
            src/simulator.h

Copyright:  Gary K. Chen <gchen98@gmail.com>

Source:     https://github.com/gchen98/macs

Reference:  Chen GK, Marjoram P, Wall JD (2009) Fast and flexible
            simulation of DNA sequence data. Genome Research 19:136-142.
            https://doi.org/10.1101/gr.083634.108

License:    Apache License, Version 2.0. The full text of the license is
            given in the file inst/LICENSE-2.0.txt, which is installed
            with the package as LICENSE-2.0.txt.

The files listed above have been modified by the AlphaSimR authors. In
summary, the modifications are:

  * The MaCS command line program has been replaced by an interface that
    is called from R. Results are returned in memory rather than written
    to standard output or to temporary files, and the entry point in
    src/simulator.cpp has been rewritten accordingly.

  * Errors and messages are reported through R, so that a failed
    simulation raises an R condition instead of writing to standard
    error or terminating the process.

  * Random number seeds are supplied by R, one per chromosome, so that
    MaCS simulations can be reproduced with set.seed() and several
    chromosomes can be simulated in parallel.

  * Headers, includes and declarations have been adjusted so that the
    code compiles as part of an R package.

  * The simulation has been made faster and less memory hungry, without
    changing the model being simulated. The cumulative branch lengths of
    the current local tree are now held in an index, so the edge carrying
    a crossover or a mutation is found by binary search instead of by
    walking the tree. An edge marked for deletion now releases its slot
    immediately instead of waiting to be drawn at random. Pruning the
    ancestral recombination graph no longer rescans edges it has already
    deleted. A segregating site is no longer copied through a temporary
    object on its way into the result, and the results of a simulation
    are moved rather than copied when they are returned to R.

  * When AlphaSimR asks for a limited number of segregating sites, MaCS
    now draws that sample while it simulates the chromosome, using
    reservoir sampling, rather than generating every site and discarding
    most of them afterwards. The retained sites are a simple random
    sample of all the sites that were generated, which is the sample
    AlphaSimR previously drew after the fact, and every site is retained
    whenever fewer are generated than were asked for. Sites are returned
    in position order. Allele frequency ascertainment needs the
    frequency of every site, so it disables this behaviour.

  * These changes alter the sequence in which random numbers are drawn,
    so a given seed does not reproduce the output of earlier versions.

Modifications are copyright the AlphaSimR authors and are released under
the Apache License, Version 2.0, on the same terms as the original.
